Problem Statement 
Given n pairs of parentheses, write a function to generate all combinations of well-formed 
(valid) parentheses.

Eg: 1
Input: n = 3
Output: ["((()))", "(()())", "(())()", "()(())", "()()()"]

Eg: 2
Input: n = 1
Output: ["()"]

Brute Force: 
The simplest way to solve this is to generate all possible sequences of length 2n using ( and ).
Once a sequence is generated, we check if it is valid (well-formed).

Algo:
Recursively add either a ( or ) until the string reaches length 2n.
Once the length is 2n use a validator function to check if the parentheses are balanced.
The validator ensures that at no point does the count of ) exceed ( and at the end of the string 
the counts are exactly equal (balance is 0)

//Check if a string has valid parentheses 
bool isValid(string s) {
    int balance = 0;
    for (char c : s) {
        if (c == '(') balance++;
        else balance--;

        //If closing brackets exceed opening brackets at any point 
        if (balance < 0) return false;
    }
    return balance == 0; //if balance == 0 then it will return true else it will return false (for condition balance != 0)
}

void generateAll(string curr, int n, vector<string>& res) {
    //Base case: string reached max length (2 * n)
    if (curr.length() == 2 * n) {
        if (isValid(curr)) {
            res.push_back(curr);
        }
        return;
    }
}

//Generate all combinations 
generateAll(curr + '(', n, res);
generateAll(curr + ')', n, res);

vector<string> generateParenthesis(int n) {
    vector<string> res;
    generateAll("", n, res);
    return res;
}

int main() {
    int n = 3;
    vector<string> result = generateParenthesis(n);
    for (string s : result) cout << s << endl;
    return 0;
}

TC: O(2^(2n) * n)
There are 2^2n possible strings of length 2n.
For each string the validation step takes O(n) time.

SC: O(n) 
For the recursive call stack depth (the maximum depth is 2n)
This does not include the space used to store the valid results.


Optimal (Backtracking)
Instead of generating all possible strings and throwing away the invalid ones at the end, we can 
build the strings strategically so we only generate valid combinations. 
We do this by keeping track of the count of opening and closing parentheses.

Algo:
1. Start with an empty string and initialize counters: open = 0 and close = 0;

2. Rule 1: We can only add an opening parenthesis ( if we have not used all n of them yet 
    if (open < n)

3. Rule 2: We can only add a closing parenthesis ) if doing so would not make it unmatched 
    if (close < open)

4. Base Case: If the string length reaches 2n, its guaranteed to be valid, so we add it to our result.

void backtrack(string curr, int open, int close, int n, vector<string>& res) {
    //Base case: reached max length, string is guaranteed valid 
    if (curr.length() == 2 * n) {
        res.push_back(curr);
        return;
    }

    //Add '(' if we have not used all 'n' opening brackets 
    if (open < n) {
        backtrack(curr + '(', open + 1, close, n, res);
    }

    //Add ')' if there are unmatched opening brackets available 
    if (close < open) {
        backtrack(curr + ')', open, close + 1, n, res);
    }
}

vector<string> generateParenthesis(int n) {
    vector<string> res;
    backtrack("", 0, 0, n, res);
    return res;
}

int main() {
    int n = 3;
    vector<string> result = generateParenthesis(n);
    for (string s : result) cout << s << endl;
    return 0;
}

TC: O(C(n) * n)
Here C(n) is the nth Catalan number which calculates the exact number of valid parenthesis combinations.
The formula for C(n) is roughly 4^n / (n * sqrt(n))
Since each of the C(n) strings takes O(n) time to build, the total time is proportional to O(C(n) * n)

SC: O(n)
For the maximum recursion stack depth (which is 2n) 
Storing the answers take O(C(n) * n) space.

